#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124177 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf35c502-5a22-5953-9158-b2464d28c09f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3377" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v$.\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\overrightarrow{AB} & = \\langle  1 , 4 \\rangle  - \\langle  0 , 1 \\rangle \\\\  & = \\langle  1 , 3 \\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & = \\frac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\Vert  \\overrightarrow{AB} \\Vert} \\\\  & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2} + {3}^{2}}} \\langle  1 , 3 \\rangle \\\\  & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\langle  1 , 3 \\rangle\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla g(x, y) &= \\langle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(3x^{2} + 4xy), \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(3x^{2} + 4xy)\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\langle 6x + 4y, 4x\\rangle  \\\\\n\\nabla g(0, 1) &= \\langle 6 \\cdot 0 + 4 \\cdot 1, 4 \\cdot 0\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\langle 4, 0\\rangle \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_{u}^{\\prime }(0, 1) &= \\nabla g(0, 1) \\cdot u \\\\\n&= \\langle 4, 0\\rangle  \\cdot \\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\rangle  \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124178 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc1e4548-ce72-5618-86f4-b68b46b50550" :question/math-academy-id "e-3381" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- \\nabla f(x, y) &= -\\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(\\ln (x^{2}y))i - \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(\\ln (x^{2}y))j \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{x^{2}y} \\cdot 2xyi - \\frac{1}{x^{2}y} \\cdot x^{2}j \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{x}i - \\frac{1}{y}j \\\\\n- \\nabla f(2, 1) &= -\\frac{2}{2}i - \\frac{1}{1}j \\\\\n&=-i - j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $-\\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124179 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1eea4610-508a-5437-802e-9d009b777e82" :question/math-academy-id "e-3382" :question/problem "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right)$. What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\n$$\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } g ( x , y ) & = \\langle  \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x} ( \\ln   ( \\sqrt{x {y}^{3}} ) ) , \\, \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y} ( \\ln   ( \\sqrt{x {y}^{3}} ) ) \\rangle \\\\  & = \\langle  \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x {y}^{3}}} \\cdot  \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2 \\sqrt{x {y}^{3}}} , \\, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x {y}^{3}}} \\cdot  \\frac{3 x {y}^{2}}{2 \\sqrt{x {y}^{3}}} \\rangle \\\\  & = \\langle  \\frac{1}{2 x} , \\, \\frac{3}{2 y} \\rangle \\\\ \\mathrm{\\nabla } g ( \\frac{1}{2} , \\frac{3}{2} ) & = \\langle  \\frac{1}{2 \\cdot  ( \\frac{1}{2} )} , \\frac{3}{2 \\cdot  ( \\frac{3}{2} )} \\rangle \\\\  & = \\langle  1 , 1 \\rangle\\end{array}$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\n$$\n\nin the direction of\n\n$$\n\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Vert \\nabla g(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{3}{2})\\Vert  &= \\Vert \\langle 1, 1\\rangle \\Vert  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d25303e8-4171-5767-b635-3131d40c76af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 2, 2, 1\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122309} {:answer/id #uuid "08e1c8d0-f6c8-5cc2-8b3c-78b61e4d157a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle  - \\pi ,-1, \\pi \\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122310} {:answer/id #uuid "2614ff57-8b46-5079-829c-9df869fffcbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle  - 2\\pi ,-2, - \\pi \\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122311} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0e731e-c0a5-53fe-a33d-b382531c5c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 2\\pi , 2, \\pi \\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122312} {:answer/id #uuid "61fb4039-081f-58b3-b342-d1ff007bbd27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle \\pi , 2, \\pi \\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d0e731e-c0a5-53fe-a33d-b382531c5c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 2\\pi , 2, \\pi \\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122312} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d1a239c-67ce-5aad-88d2-8f3538d62136" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a412c3a-6b92-5f8b-8cf9-ec76f9ca97fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-36305" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x, y, z) = y^{2} + \\sin (2\\pi x + \\pi z)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1, 1, 2)$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $$\\nabla g=\\langle2\\pi\\cos(2\\pi x+\\pi z),2y,\\pi\\cos(2\\pi x+\\pi z)\\rangle.$$ At $(1,1,2)$ the cosine is $\\cos4\\pi=1$, so $\\nabla g=\\langle2\\pi,2,\\pi\\rangle$. This nonzero gradient points in the direction of greatest increase."}]}
